AU  - Siguel, E
TI  - Deficiencies and abnormalities of essential fats in gastrointestinal and coronary artery disease
T2  - JOURNAL OF CLINICAL LIGAND ASSAY
AB  - The essential fats omega 3 and omega 6 are two families of polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs) consisting of the essential fatty acids (EFAs) and their fatty acid derivatives. The EFAs linoleic acid (omega 6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega 3) have long been known to be essential. A commonly used measure of EFA deficiency (EFAD) is an increase in the ratio 20:3 omega 9/20:4 omega 6-the ratio of the concentrations of triene (20:3 omega 9) to tetraene (20:4 omega 6) (T/T). Other measures include shifts in the formation of omega 7 and omega 9 derivatives, particularly 16:1 omega 7, as well as the percent and concentrations of PUFAs in:plasma and red blood cells (RBCs).
   Patients with chronic gastrointestinal disease (GID) and coronary artery disease (CAD) exhibit similar shifts in fatty acid metabolism which indicate EFAD, namely an increase in the percent of omega 7 and omega 9 fatty acids in plasma, as well as a decrease in the percentages of PUFAs. As a general rule, the shift is much greater in patients with GID than CAD. CAD patients(a disease usually associated with excessive caloric intake) tend to have much greater concentration of all fatty acids, including PUFAs, than do GID patients (who have malabsorption). The fatty acid metabolic profile in other conditions tends to resemble one of these two opposite types of patients.
   Modulation of EFA metabolites with diet after careful evaluation of fatty acid abnormalities provides a highly powerful tool to treat a wide range of disorders, including complications of diabetes mellitus, cystic fibrosis, Crohn's disease, dermatitis, brain dysfunction, behavioral disturbances, depression, and neurological diseases. Most patients need supplements of omega 3s and omega 6s, often with more w3s to correct a proportionately greater deficiency in omega 3s than omega 6s. Because the essential fats are macronutrients, large amounts are required to achieve biological effects. However, because essential fats are stored and not readily discarded las are many minerals or vitamins), optimal intake requires monitoring body levels before and after therapy, particularly when large doses of essential fats are used.
