TI  - n3-Fatty acids:: Health effects and opportunities to increase intake
T2  - AGRO FOOD INDUSTRY HI-TECH
AB  - Cardiovascular disease (CVD) is the leading cause of death in Europe, the US and a major part of Asia. A variety of risk factors are associated with CVD, including high cholesterol levels, high plasma levels of homocysteine, hypertension, diabetes, low HDL-cholesterol levels and low levels of antioxidants, most of them influenced by diet.
   In spite of the many foodstuffs enriched in n-3 fatty acids available in the market, the bioavailability and health effects derived from regular consumption of those products remain as a challenge in most cases. Only very few studies have been conducted to assess whether health benefits may come from consumption of foods to which n-3 fatty acids have been added.
   We have used milk as a vehicle for n-3 PUFA, oleic acid, and vitamins E, B6, and folic acid supplementation, and further studied the effects of this product on risk factors for young (25-45 y) healthy subjects with cardiovascular disease. In this study, compared to other studies, we have used very small doses of n-3 fatty acids and oleic acid. We have found that 2 months consumption of the n-3 enriched milk significantly reduced risk factors for cardiovascular disease.
   The intake of a range of foods enriched with alpha-linolenic acid, EPA and DHA can be used to achieve desirable biochemical effects without the ingestion of supplements or changing dietary habits. Although the market of functional foods is constantly increasing, more research is needed to evaluate to what extend consumption of enriched foods produce health benefits, an area of research that is very rapidly developing.
