TI  - Omega-3-Acid Ethyl Esters Reduces Small Dense Low Density Lipoproteins Regardless of the Baseline Lipid Profile
T2  - CURRENT ADVANCES IN HEART DISEASE
CP  - 14th World Congress on Heart Disease
AB  - The underlying metabolic cause of coronary heart disease (CH 1)) in many patients is not high cholesterol One of the most common metabolic contributors to coronary artery disease is the atherogenic lipoproteins profile. Regarding, these data we investigated the effect of omega-3-acid ethyl esters on the atherogenic lipoproteins profile. Out of 118 subjects tested by quantimetrix lipoprotein (TM) system; thirty five patients (27 males and 8 females) were included in our study. All the included patients fell into a distinct atherogenic lipid profile (type B LDL phenotype) and with normal total and LDL cholesterol (C) levels. All included patients were given omega-3- acid ethyl esters 90% at 2 gram/day (gm/d) for 8 weeks (wk). After 8-wk of treatment, omega-3- acid ethyl esters 90% (at 2 2m/d) significantly reduced levels of small dense (sd) LDL particles and mean LDL score and was well tolerated.
