TI  - Omega-3 fatty acids and resistance of the myocardium to ischemia-induced ventricular arrhythmias
T2  - ADAPTATION BIOLOGY AND MEDICINE, VOL 3: NEW FRONTIERS
CP  - 6th World Congress of the International-Society-for-Adaptive-Medicine
AB  - Animal experiments have clearly indicated that a diet rich in saturated fatty acids is associated with a high incidence of ischemia- and reperfusion- induced ventricular arrhythmias, whereas dietary fatty acids of either the (n-6) or (n-3) family reduce that risk. In humans, existing evidence suggests that an intake of (n-3) fatty acids (about I g daily), in the form of supplements or, alternatively, by eating at least two large (about 200 g) servings of fatty fish per week, will help to prevent sudden cardiac death (SCD). Despite a possible beneficial effect on the risk of SCD, (n-6) fatty acids should not be recommended because of a probable negative effect on other cause of death. Experimental, clinical and epidemiological studies, and randomized trials, did clearly demonstrate that (n-3) fatty acids reduce the risk of SCD. Their clinical use is now encouraged and their prescription should be logically included in the treatment and secondary prevention of CHD. The hypothesis that they may prevent ventricular arrhythmia and SCD in patients at high risk (for instance in secondary prevention of SCD) is currently being tested in specific ongoing trials.
